WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.600
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.450
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.120
I'm Priyanka.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.890
And in this episode,
we welcome back

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.220
Jen Person to talk about more
mobile app backend service

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.331
solutions.

00:00:12.331 --> 00:00:16.259
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:22.570
Good morning, Jen.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.097
Welcome to "Get
Cooking in Cloud."

00:00:24.097 --> 00:00:25.305
JEN PERSON: Thanks, Priyanka.

00:00:25.305 --> 00:00:27.472
I'm always happy to be here.

00:00:27.472 --> 00:00:29.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So last
time we were on this show,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.393
you shared a mobile app backend
recipe solution using Firebase.

00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:34.060
JEN PERSON: Yep.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.710
And I explained
that Firebase solves

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.068
a lot of the mobile
app backend problems.

00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:40.110
But if you need to make
some changes server side,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.370
I hinted that you need
to add to Firebase

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.603
to make a complete solution.

00:00:43.603 --> 00:00:45.520
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So I
can't wait any longer.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.073
Tell me what we're
cooking up today.

00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:47.990
JEN PERSON: All right.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:52.330
So here is our recipe
from the last episode.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.345
And here's today's ingredients.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.720
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Ah, of course.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.790
App Engine Standard Environment.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.410
Well, App Engine
Standard Environment

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.450
is an app platform
that monitors,

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.060
updates, and scales the
hosting environment.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.090
All you need to do is write your
mobile backend service code.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.230
If your app uses Firebase
for automatic real-time data

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.230
synchronization, you can
extend your Firebase solution

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.590
using App Engine Standard
Environment to process user

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.000
data or orchestrate events.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.610
For instance, in my game
"Priyanka's Kitchen,"

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.170
I use App Engine to compute
user ranks based on scores,

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.750
then write the updated
ranks to Firebase.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.250
JEN PERSON: That's
a perfect example

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.110
of how this recipe can be used.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.390
I recommend this recipe
for Firebase apps

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.990
that need a backend service to
modify the synchronized data

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.090
and backend services
that run periodically

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.450
to process or analyze Firebase
data, just like the ranking

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.180
system in "Priyanka's Kitchen."

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: App Engine
Standard Environment certainly

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.320
adds some seasoning to
the Firebase recipe.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.360
But of course, no
one recipe will

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.670
suit every discerning palate.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.700
So tell me, Jen, when
would you not recommend

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.785
using this recipe?

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.160
JEN PERSON: I
don't recommend you

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.380
use today's recipe for
backend services that

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.620
call native binaries,
write to the file system,

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.193
or make other system calls.

00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:09.610
I also don't
recommend this if you

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.580
require persistent connections
to Firebase, because App Engine

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.550
Standard Environment reclaims
socket connections after just

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:16.833
two minutes.

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:18.250
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
But I'm betting

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.040
that you have a design that
can work for those use cases,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:21.903
too, right?

00:02:21.903 --> 00:02:22.570
JEN PERSON: Yep.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.670
And I have it right here.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.700
But you'll have to wait
till the next episode

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.990
to find out what it is!

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.840
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Oh, I am on
the edge of my seat here, Jen.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.730
Thanks again for coming on.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.700
And it's always a pleasure to
spend time on Cloud kitchen

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.520
with you.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.570
JEN PERSON: Any time.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.690
I can't wait to come
back to your kitchen.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.495
I just love the smell
of freshly cooked cloud.

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:45.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
that's all for today

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.600
on "Get Cooking in Cloud."

